.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt NDI_IRM_CREATE 9F
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ndi_irm_create ,
.Nm ndi_irm_destroy ,
.Nm ndi_irm_resize_pool
.Nd manage interrupt resource pools
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/sunndi.h
.In sys/ddi_intr_impl.h
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_irm_create
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "ddi_irm_params_t *paramsp"
.Fa "ddi_irm_pool_t **pool_retp"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_irm_destroy
.Fa "ddi_irm_pool_t *poolp"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_irm_resize_pool
.Fa "ddi_irm_pool_t *poolp"
.Fa "uint_t newsize"
.Fc
.Sh INTERFACE LEVEL
illumos specific
.Pq Consolidation Private .
.Sh PARAMETERS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va dip
Nexus supplying the interrupt resources.
.It Va paramsp
Valid parameter structure describing types and capacity.
.It Va pool_retp
Output address for the new pool.
.It Va poolp
Existing IRM pool.
.It Va newsize
New total vector capacity.
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An interrupt resource management pool describes interrupt vectors supplied
by a nexus and shared among its consumers.
\fBndi_irm_create()\fP allocates a pool owned by \fBdip\fP, registers it with IRM,
and returns it in \fB*pool_retp\fP.
IRM must be enabled.
The caller supplies these members of \fBddi_irm_params_t\fP:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va iparams_types
Nonzero mask of interrupt types supported by the pool, using
\fBDDI_INTR_TYPE_*\fP values.
.It Va iparams_total
Total number of vectors; must be at least one.
.El
.Pp
The parameter values are copied and the input structure can be reused after
creation.
The returned pool is managed through IRM interfaces; callers must not modify
its internal fields directly.
.Pp
\fBndi_irm_resize_pool()\fP changes the pool's total size.
Growing a pool can schedule asynchronous rebalancing.
Shrinking below the current reservations performs a synchronous rebalance
to reclaim vectors.
If enough vectors cannot be reclaimed, the previous pool size is restored
and the call fails.
Changes already made to consumer allocations need not be undone immediately.
.Pp
\fBndi_irm_destroy()\fP removes the pool and stops its balancing thread.
All reserved vectors must have been released before destruction.
Callers must prevent new users during destruction and must not use the pool
after successful destruction.
.Sh CONTEXT
These functions may be called from user or kernel context.
They may sleep.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP on success.
Creation returns \fBNDI_FAILURE\fP when IRM is disabled or parameters are invalid.
Resizing returns \fBNDI_FAILURE\fP when IRM is disabled or rebalancing cannot
satisfy the new size, and \fBNDI_EINVAL\fP for a null pool.
Destruction returns \fBNDI_BUSY\fP for outstanding reservations and
\fBNDI_FAILURE\fP for a null pool.
Invalid pointers and outstanding reservations also violate assertions in
debug kernels; callers must meet the documented prerequisites.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ddi_cb_register 9F ,
.Xr ddi_intr_alloc 9F ,
.Xr sunndi 9F
